---

<BaseLayout
  title="Career Change at 30 India: The Real EMI, Family, and Timing Math"
  description="Career change at 30 India means real EMI math, marriage-timing pressure, and one of three paths: upskill, pivot, or build something. Real runway numbers and a fit test inside."
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <Nav activePage="blog" />

  <article id="itm-article">
    <header class="hero">
      <div class="hero-bg">
        <div class="orb orb-gold"></div>
        <div class="orb orb-teal"></div>
        <div class="hero-grid"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="container hero-inner">
        <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <a href="/">Home</a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span>
          <a href="/blog">Blog</a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span>
          <a href="/blog/career-change">Career Change</a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span>
          <span aria-current="page">Career Change at 30 India</span>
        </nav>

        <div class="hero-meta">
          <span class="pill hero-pill"><span class="pill-dot"></span>EMI, family, and timing decision map</span>
          <span class="date-pill">Published: {publishedDateLabel}</span>
          <a class="date-pill date-pill--link" href={authorUrl}>By {authorName}</a>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-grid-main">
          <div class="hero-copy">
            <h1>Career change at 30 India: the real math on EMI, family timing, and the 3 real paths</h1>
            <p class="line strong">
              A career change at 30 in India is not the same decision a fresher makes. You are usually carrying a home loan EMI or the start of one, a marriage timeline your family keeps bringing up, and years of sunk cost in a lane you already know well — none of which a 22-year-old choosing a first job has to weigh. That does not make 30 too late. It means the plan has to run on real numbers, a real family conversation, and one of three tested paths — upskilling in place, a full pivot, or building something of your own — instead of a leap of faith. Done with a real skill plan behind it, a change at 30 can also put you on a faster track to financial freedom than staying in a stalled lane out of caution.
            </p>
            <p class="line">Is everyone around you already "settled" while you are still figuring out if this job is even right?</p>
            <p class="line">Is a home loan EMI or a wedding fund making the whole idea feel financially reckless?</p>
            <p class="line">Is a parent or relative treating "you're 30 now" like a countdown timer on your career, not just your marriage?</p>
            <p class="line">None of that confusion means you are behind. It means nobody has laid out the actual constraints and the actual paths side by side for you yet.</p>
            <p class="line">This is that map — the runway math, the family conversation, and the fit test, before you spend a year or a lakh of rupees on the wrong path.</p>

          </div>

          <aside class="hero-board card">
            <div class="board-kicker">What actually changes at 30</div>
            <div class="board-row">
              <span>1</span>
              <p>A real EMI or savings target sits under the decision, not just interest.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="board-row">
              <span>2</span>
              <p>Family and marriage timing enter the conversation whether you invite them or not.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="board-row">
              <span>3</span>
              <p>You have proof and sunk cost a fresher does not — use it, do not discard it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="board-row">
              <span>4</span>
              <p>The runway is shorter, so the test has to be smaller and faster.</p>
            </div>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="container section">
      <div class="key-takeaways">
        <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Career change at 30 India is common, not rare: India-specific LinkedIn survey data found 57% of Indian professionals would consider career pivoting, and 30-34 shows up as one of the age bands most actively seeking a change.</li>
          <li>The real blocker is rarely age. It is usually an EMI that already eats 30%+ of take-home income, an unfunded runway, or an unstructured family conversation about timing.</li>
          <li>Run a 3-6 month emergency fund minimum before any pay dip, and 12 months if you are moving toward freelancing or a business with variable income.</li>
          <li>There are three real paths, not one: upskill and switch lanes inside your current employer, a full external pivot, or building something of your own — each with a different runway and risk profile.</li>
          <li>A costed, honest plan — target direction, runway, and a real fallback — moves both the money math and the family conversation forward faster than either avoiding the topic or quitting on conviction alone.</li>
          <li>Do not discard the first decade. Combine its domain knowledge with AI fluency, communication, and proof in the next lane; an adjacent specialist, product, consulting, freelance, or ownership path usually creates a better ceiling than starting from zero.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="cta-pair">
        <a class="btn-gold pulse" href={guidanceUrl}>Book continuous career guidance</a>
        <a class="btn-outline cta-assessment-lite" href={assessmentsUrl}>Use a free assessment as a quick first check</a>
      </div>

      <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Jump to section">
        <span class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</span>
        <div class="jump-nav__grid">
          <a href="#why-different">Why a career change at 30 India feels different</a>
          <a href="#fit-check">The 4-Checkpoint Protocol for this decision</a>
          <a href="#fresher-vs-30">Fresher's freedom vs your constraints at 30</a>
          <a href="#money-math">The real EMI and runway math</a>
          <a href="#three-paths">3 real paths: upskill, pivot, or build</a>
          <a href="#family-marriage">The family and marriage-timing conversation</a>
          <a href="#skill-stack">The skill stack that gets you taken seriously at 30</a>
          <a href="#test-plan">Your test-before-you-leap plan</a>
          <a href="#mistakes">Mistakes that cost a year and a lot of money</a>
          <a href="#faqs">FAQs</a>
        </div>
      </nav>
    </section>

    <section id="why-different" class="container section">
      <h2>Why a career change at 30 India feels different from any career decision before it</h2>
      <p class="line">
        A career change at 30 in India rarely arrives as a single dramatic moment. It usually builds up the way most mid-career discomfort does: a role that no longer matches what you actually studied for, interests that have quietly drifted away from what the job demands, a pay band that has stopped moving while newer joiners close the gap, or skills that were once your edge starting to feel dated. None of that is a personal failure. It is what most working adults eventually run into once the first decade of "take whatever job comes" settles into "is this actually who I want to be for the next thirty years."
      </p>
      <p class="line">
        What makes 30 specifically different in India is not the career question itself — it is what sits around it. Community discussion around this exact age bracket consistently names three things that a 22-year-old fresher simply does not have to factor in: marriage, kids, and a house loan. Those three commitments do not make a career change wrong. They make it a decision with real fixed costs attached, which is exactly why a generic "follow your passion" article fails you here.
      </p>

      <div class="card highlight-card">
        <h3>What the data says about career change at 30 in India</h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Signal</th>
                <th>What it means for your decision</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Signal">57% of Indian professionals would consider career pivoting, per LinkedIn's Career Pathways India survey of over 5,000 professionals</td>
                <td data-label="What it means for your decision">You are part of a majority sentiment, not an outlier making an unusual choice.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Signal">51% of professionals cite pay and designation impact as the reason they hold back from switching</td>
                <td data-label="What it means for your decision">Money is the honest blocker for most people, which is exactly why the EMI and runway math below deserves real attention, not vague optimism.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Signal">23% of employees aged 30-34 are actively seeking a career change, one of the higher rates across age bands</td>
                <td data-label="What it means for your decision">This is a normal stage-of-career pattern, not a sign that something has gone wrong specifically for you.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Signal">Among 30-34 year olds, 32% have shifted careers once and 21% twice, while 32% have made no career move at all</td>
                <td data-label="What it means for your decision">Not moving is also common — the goal here is a deliberate decision either way, not decision paralysis.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="line">
        None of this means you should switch just because the data says many people consider it. It means the itch you are feeling has a name, a pattern, and a large enough population of people working through the same math that you do not have to invent the decision framework from scratch.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="fit-check" class="container section">
      <h2>The 4-Checkpoint Protocol: run this before you change anything</h2>
      <p class="line">
        Before you pick a direction or spend money on a course, certification, or business idea, run your situation through <strong>The 4-Checkpoint Protocol</strong> — Biology, Context, Market, and Survival — adapted here for exactly what changes for you at 30 in India.
      </p>

      <div class="protocol-stack">
        <div class="protocol-row">
          <span class="protocol-num">1</span>
          <div class="protocol-body">
            <h3>Biology: is this fatigue with the role, or fatigue with change itself?</h3>
            <p>A demanding quarter can feel identical to a real misfit from the inside. Before you act, separate "I am tired right now" from "I have felt disconnected from this work for over a year, across different managers and different projects." The second pattern is structural. The first usually resolves with rest, a role adjustment, or a manager change.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="protocol-row">
          <span class="protocol-num">2</span>
          <div class="protocol-body">
            <h3>Context: what does your specific money and family picture actually allow?</h3>
            <p>A 30-year-old with no dependents and a small EMI can absorb a leaner few months while testing a new direction. A 30-year-old close to a wedding, supporting parents, or carrying a large EMI has a shorter runway and needs a plan sequenced to protect income while testing, not risk it. Your context decides which of the three paths further down is realistic right now.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="protocol-row">
          <span class="protocol-num">3</span>
          <div class="protocol-body">
            <h3>Market: is there real demand for the direction you want, where you actually are?</h3>
            <p>A field that looks hot on LinkedIn nationally can still be thin in your specific city or your specific network. Check job postings, salary bands, and real people already doing the work near you before you commit money or time, not just the headline trend.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="protocol-row">
          <span class="protocol-num">4</span>
          <div class="protocol-body">
            <h3>Survival: does the new direction hold up over the next decade, not just this year?</h3>
            <p>At 30, you likely have 30-plus working years ahead. A direction chosen only because it pays well today, with no real skill depth or judgment layer behind it, can plateau or get automated faster than the ten-year runway you need it to survive. Favour directions that ask for real skill and judgment, not just current market heat.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="line">
        If your honest answers point toward "yes, this is structural, my context can support a tested move, the market wants this near me, and it survives the next decade" — move to the paths below. If two or more checkpoints are shaky, that is not a reason to abandon the idea. It is a reason to test it smaller and cheaper first, which is exactly what the test plan further down is built for.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="fresher-vs-30" class="container section">
      <h2>Fresher's freedom vs your constraints at 30</h2>
      <p class="line">
        Most career-change advice online is written for someone with no dependents, no EMI, and a decade to recover from a wrong turn. That advice is not wrong — it is just not written for you. Comparing the two situations side by side is the fastest way to see exactly what needs to change in your plan.
      </p>

      <div class="grid-two">
        <div class="card accent-teal-card">
          <h3>What a fresher can afford to do</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Try three unrelated fields over a few years without a real financial penalty.</li>
            <li>Take an unpaid internship or a low-paying entry role purely to learn.</li>
            <li>Move cities or take a pay cut with minimal pushback from family, since expectations are still forming.</li>
            <li>Treat a "wrong" first job as normal, not as a story they have to explain to anyone.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card accent-gold-card">
          <h3>What you are actually working with at 30</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Real sunk cost: years of domain knowledge, a network, and a track record in your current lane that a fresher simply does not have.</li>
            <li>Fixed monthly costs: EMI, insurance, sometimes dependents, that need a funded runway before any income dip.</li>
            <li>A marriage or family timeline that others feel entitled to comment on, whether or not you invited the opinion.</li>
            <li>A narrower window to test before pressure — financial or social — pushes you toward a decision either way.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card honest-take">
        <p class="honest-label">Honest take</p>
        <p class="line">
          The years you already have are not a disadvantage to erase. They are proof of work a fresher cannot show: a track record, a network, and domain fluency you can carry into an adjacent direction instead of starting from zero. The real skill at 30 is not "exploring broadly" like a 22-year-old — it is picking a direction fast, testing it cheaply, and using what you already built as leverage rather than treating it as wasted time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="money-math" class="container section">
      <h2>The real EMI and runway math behind a career change at 30 India</h2>
      <p class="line">
        This is the part most career-change content skips, and it is usually the actual reason a switch stalls or succeeds. Run these numbers honestly before you commit to anything.
      </p>

      <div class="card highlight-card">
        <h3>The three numbers to calculate before you move</h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Number</th>
                <th>What financial guidance recommends</th>
                <th>Why it matters at 30</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Number">Emergency fund / runway</td>
                <td data-label="What financial guidance recommends">3-6 months of essential expenses as a baseline; 6-9 months if you expect a pay dip; up to 12 months if moving into freelancing or a business</td>
                <td data-label="Why it matters at 30">This is the buffer that lets you test a new direction without panic-driven decisions three months in.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Number">EMI-to-take-home ratio</td>
                <td data-label="What financial guidance recommends">Comfortable affordability generally sits under 30-35% of take-home income, even though banks may approve EMIs up to 40-55% of gross income</td>
                <td data-label="Why it matters at 30">If your EMI already sits near the higher end, an unpaid break is high-risk; sequence the change around staying employed while you test.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td data-label="Number">Take-home vs gross gap</td>
                <td data-label="What financial guidance recommends">After tax and PF deductions, an EMI that looks like 40% of gross salary is often closer to 55-60% of actual take-home pay</td>
                <td data-label="Why it matters at 30">Always run affordability math on take-home income, not the salary figure on your offer letter.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="table-note">These are general financial-planning benchmarks from Indian personal-finance sources, not a substitute for reviewing your own numbers with a financial advisor.</p>
      </div>

      <p class="line">
        Notice what these numbers do not say: they do not say "don't do it." They say build the buffer first, and sequence the change so your fixed costs are covered while you test. A career change funded by an emptied emergency fund and a maxed-out EMI is a different, much riskier decision than the same change funded by six months of covered expenses and a plan to keep earning while you test.
      </p>

      <div class="cta-pair">
        <a class="btn-gold pulse" href={guidanceUrl}>Book continuous career guidance</a>
        <a class="btn-outline cta-assessment-lite" href={assessmentsUrl}>Use a free assessment as a quick first check</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="three-paths" class="container section">
      <h2>3 real paths for a career change at 30 India (not one blurry "switch")</h2>
      <p class="line">
        "Career change" gets treated online like one door. At 30 in India, with a real EMI and family timeline in the picture, it is actually three different doors with three different risk profiles. Pick the one your Context checkpoint above can genuinely support right now.
      </p>

      <div class="track-grid">
        <div class="track-card">
          <span class="track-badge">Path 1</span>
          <h3>Upskill and switch lanes in place</h3>
          <p>You add a new skill layer and move into an adjacent function or team inside your current company or industry, without a full income gap.</p>
          <div class="sub-row sub-row--fit">
            <span class="sub-row-label">Best for</span>
            <p>A high EMI, a wedding on the horizon, or dependents to support right now.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="sub-row sub-row--caution">
            <span class="sub-row-label">Watch out</span>
            <p>Progress can feel slower since you are not making a clean break; it needs deliberate, visible proof-building, not just quiet studying.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="tag-row">
            <span class="tag-pill">Lowest income risk</span>
            <span class="tag-pill">Uses existing network</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="track-card">
          <span class="track-badge">Path 2</span>
          <h3>A full external pivot</h3>
          <p>You change field, function, or industry through a new employer, sometimes alongside a certification or short course to close the gap.</p>
          <div class="sub-row sub-row--fit">
            <span class="sub-row-label">Best for</span>
            <p>A funded 6-9 month runway, a tested direction, and a family conversation that has already happened.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="sub-row sub-row--caution">
            <span class="sub-row-label">Watch out</span>
            <p>Your existing network mostly does not transfer to a new field; expect to rebuild credibility with new people, not just a new title.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="tag-row">
            <span class="tag-pill">Clean break</span>
            <span class="tag-pill">Needs a funded runway</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="track-card">
          <span class="track-badge">Path 3</span>
          <h3>Build something of your own</h3>
          <p>You start freelancing, consulting, or a small business built on domain expertise you already have, rather than switching employers.</p>
          <div class="sub-row sub-row--fit">
            <span class="sub-row-label">Best for</span>
            <p>Real domain expertise, a starter client or two already identified, and a 12-month runway for variable income.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="sub-row sub-row--caution">
            <span class="sub-row-label">Watch out</span>
            <p>Income is the least predictable of the three paths in year one; this is the path most likely to fail without the longer runway.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="tag-row">
            <span class="tag-pill">Highest ceiling</span>
            <span class="tag-pill">Needs the longest runway</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="line">
        None of these three is universally "the right one." A path chosen purely for prestige or purely to escape a bad manager, without checking it against your actual runway, is how a well-intentioned career change turns into a financial and family crisis instead of an upgrade.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="family-marriage" class="container section">
      <h2>The family and marriage-timing conversation nobody prepares you for</h2>
      <p class="line">
        In India specifically, 30 rarely arrives as just a career milestone. It usually arrives with parallel questions about marriage, and a career change announced at the same time as those questions tends to get read as instability, even when it is the opposite — a deliberate, researched decision.
      </p>

      <div class="grid-two">
        <div class="card accent-violet-card">
          <h3>Why family resistance shows up here specifically</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>A pay dip or title change is the visible part of the story; the reasoning behind it usually is not, unless you explain it directly.</li>
            <li>Marriage conversations in Indian families are frequently tied to "settled" career status, so a switch can look like it is delaying both, even when it is not.</li>
            <li>Most resistance is a trust gap about the unknown, not a genuine rejection of your judgment.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card accent-teal-card">
          <h3>What actually closes the trust gap</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Name the actual target direction, not a vague "I want something different."</li>
            <li>Show the funded runway explicitly: how many months are covered, and how EMI or wedding costs are accounted for.</li>
            <li>Show the fallback: if the test does not work in the window you set, you return to your current lane having lost only the tested time, not your main income.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="line">
        A costed, honest plan gets a fundamentally different reaction than a mood-driven announcement. You are not asking for permission — you are giving people who care about your stability the information they need to stop worrying about it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="skill-stack" class="container section">
      <h2>The skill stack that gets you taken seriously at 30, not just "trying something new"</h2>
      <p class="line">
        At 30, "I'm interested in this" gets you a polite conversation. Visible proof gets you an interview, a client, or a promotion into the new lane. Build these three layers before you lean on your years of experience as your main pitch.
      </p>

      <div class="tier-container">
        <div class="tier-header tier-header--core">Core skill: the one directly required by your target direction</div>
        <div class="tier-row">
          <span class="tier-dot tier-dot--core"></span>
          <p>Pick the single skill that shows up in almost every job posting or client brief for your target direction, and get functional at it first — not a broad survey of everything adjacent to it.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tier-header tier-header--multiplier">Multiplier skill: the leverage from your existing years</div>
        <div class="tier-row">
          <span class="tier-dot tier-dot--multiplier"></span>
          <p>Your domain knowledge, stakeholder experience, or industry network from your current lane is a real edge over a fresher entering the same target direction cold. Name it explicitly in interviews and pitches instead of downplaying your "old" background.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tier-header tier-header--proof">Proof format: one documented result, not a list of intentions</div>
        <div class="tier-row">
          <span class="tier-dot tier-dot--proof"></span>
          <p>A single real project, a small paid client outcome, or a certification tied to a visible piece of work beats a resume line that only says you are "interested in" the new direction. Build the proof before you lead with the pitch.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card highlight-card">
        <p class="line strong">
          The chain that actually moves you here is simple: the right skill stack builds real proof, and real proof is what shortens the runway you need — because a direction backed by evidence gets funded and hired faster than a direction backed only by intention. Built deliberately, this is also how a career change compounds into a genuine high-income skill portfolio instead of one more job title.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="line">
        This is also the holistic approach behind ongoing career guidance done well at this stage — not matching you to a job title, but weighing your actual skill direction, your proof, your EMI and family reality, and your realistic timeline together, instead of any one of them in isolation.
      </p>

      <div class="cta-pair">
        <a class="btn-gold pulse" href={guidanceUrl}>Book continuous career guidance</a>
        <a class="btn-outline cta-assessment-lite" href={assessmentsUrl}>Use a free assessment as a quick first check</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="test-plan" class="container section">
      <h2>Your test-before-you-leap plan</h2>
      <p class="line">
        Do not quit your job, drain your emergency fund, or announce a full pivot to your family before you have tested your direction properly. Move through the steps below at whatever pace fits your job, EMI, and family situation — some people move through all four in a couple of months, others need longer, and both are fine as long as each step is done for real.
      </p>

      <div class="pilot-grid">
        <div class="card pilot-card">
          <span>Step 1</span>
          <p>Run the 4-Checkpoint Protocol honestly. Calculate your actual EMI-to-take-home ratio and your current emergency fund in months, not a rough guess.</p>
          <p>Talk to two or three people already working in your target direction — one inside your network, two outside it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card pilot-card">
          <span>Step 2</span>
          <p>Pick one of the three paths — upskill in place, full pivot, or build something — based on your Context checkpoint, not on which one sounds most exciting.</p>
          <p>Start building the core skill and one small piece of visible proof, without quitting anything yet.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card pilot-card">
          <span>Step 3</span>
          <p>Have the family conversation with a costed plan in hand: target direction, funded runway in months, and your fallback if the test does not work.</p>
          <p>If you chose the pivot or build-your-own path, start one small real test — an application loop, a first client conversation, or a paid pilot project.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card pilot-card">
          <span>Step 4</span>
          <p>Review honestly: did the market respond, did you enjoy the actual work once you tried it, and does your runway still cover the next stretch?</p>
          <p>Decide: commit further with a real financial step, adjust the path, or park it with a clearer plan for when your runway allows another test — all three are valid outcomes of a genuine test.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card highlight-card">
        <p class="line strong">
          The goal of these four steps is not a lifetime decision made this month. It is real evidence, at low cost to your EMI and your family's trust, before you make an expensive one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="mistakes" class="container section">
      <h2>Mistakes that cost a year and a lot of money at this age</h2>
      <div class="grid-two">
        <div class="card warn-card">
          <h3>Expensive, common mistakes</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Quitting first and figuring out the new direction after, with no emergency fund covering the gap.</li>
            <li>Announcing a pivot to family as a mood decision instead of a costed plan, which reads as instability even when it is not.</li>
            <li>Taking on a course loan or business investment without checking it against your actual EMI-to-take-home ratio first.</li>
            <li>Treating your years of existing experience as a liability to hide, instead of leverage to name explicitly.</li>
            <li>Choosing the highest-ceiling path (building something of your own) without the longer runway it actually needs.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card win-card">
          <h3>What to do instead</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Run the 4-Checkpoint fit check and the EMI/runway math before committing money or resigning from anything.</li>
            <li>Pick one of the three paths that matches your actual Context checkpoint, not the most exciting story.</li>
            <li>Bring a costed plan to the family conversation: direction, runway, fallback.</li>
            <li>Lead with your existing domain knowledge and network as an edge, not something to apologise for.</li>
            <li>Test small before you commit big, regardless of which path you choose.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container section">
      <h2>Source-backed reality check</h2>
      <p class="line">Do not take any career article, including this one, on faith. Check primary sources and apply your own judgment to your specific situation.</p>
      <ul class="sources">
        <li>
          LinkedIn Career Pathways India survey of over 5,000 Indian professionals on career-pivot intent and the factors holding people back.
